{"name":"Mirror Ball","hex":"#d6d4d7","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mirror ball","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A disco ball."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mirror_ball"}],"entries":[{"title":"Disco ball","blocks":[{"text":"A disco ball is a roughly spherical object that reflects light directed at it in many directions, producing a complex display. Its surface consists of hundreds or thousands of facets, nearly all of approximately the same shape and size, and each has a mirrored surface. Usually, it is mounted well above the heads of the people present, suspended from a device that causes it to rotate steadily on a vertical axis, and illuminated by spotlights, so that stationary viewers experience beams of light flashing over them, and see myriad spots of light spinning around the walls of the room."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Mirrored_Disco_Ball_with_Light_and_Motor.jpg/330px-Mirrored_Disco_Ball_with_Light_and_Motor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disco_ball"},{"title":"Mirror Ball (Neil Young album)","blocks":[{"text":"Mirror Ball is the twenty-third studio album by Canadian-American musician Neil Young, and features members of American rock band Pearl Jam. It was released on June 27, 1995, through Reprise Records. The album has been certified gold by the RIAA in the United States."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/b7/NeilYoungMirrorBall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mirror_Ball_(Neil_Young_album)"},{"title":"Mirror","blocks":[{"text":"A mirror, also known as a looking glass, is an object that reflects an image. Light that bounces off a mirror forms an image of whatever is in front of it, which is then focused through the lens of the eye or a camera. Mirrors reverse the direction of light at an angle equal to its incidence. This allows the viewer to see themselves or objects behind them, or even objects that are at an angle from them but out of their field of view, such as around a corner. Natural mirrors have existed since prehistoric times, such as the surface of water, but people have been manufacturing mirrors out of a variety of materials for thousands of years, like stone, metals, and glass. In modern mirrors, metals like silver or aluminium are often used due to their high reflectivity, applied as a thin coating on glass because of its naturally smooth and hard surface."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/52/Mirror.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mirror"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Mirrored_Disco_Ball_with_Light_and_Motor.jpg/500px-Mirrored_Disco_Ball_with_Light_and_Motor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Mirrored_Disco_Ball_with_Light_and_Motor.jpg/1280px-Mirrored_Disco_Ball_with_Light_and_Motor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Mirrored_Disco_Ball_with_Light_and_Motor.jpg/500px-Mirrored_Disco_Ball_with_Light_and_Motor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A mirrored disco ball","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMirrored_Disco_Ball_with_Light_and_Motor.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/LouisianaFiveBandstand_%28restored_version%29.jpg/500px-LouisianaFiveBandstand_%28restored_version%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/LouisianaFiveBandstand_%28restored_version%29.jpg/500px-LouisianaFiveBandstand_%28restored_version%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A mirrored ball can be seen above the bandstand in this 1919 photo of the Louisiana Five jazz band.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALouisianaFiveBandstand_(restored_version).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Disco_Ball.JPG/500px-Disco_Ball.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Disco_Ball.JPG/1280px-Disco_Ball.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Disco_Ball.JPG/500px-Disco_Ball.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A disco ball and spotlight in the Fishbowl of Currier House","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADisco_Ball.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Disco_Santa.jpg/500px-Disco_Santa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Disco_Santa.jpg/1280px-Disco_Santa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Disco_Santa.jpg/500px-Disco_Santa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Disco Santa ornament typical of 2020s disco kitsch","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADisco_Santa.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/b/b7/NeilYoungMirrorBall.jpg/250px-NeilYoungMirrorBall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/b7/NeilYoungMirrorBall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/b/b7/NeilYoungMirrorBall.jpg/250px-NeilYoungMirrorBall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANeilYoungMirrorBall.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Mirror.jpg/250px-Mirror.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/52/Mirror.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Mirror.jpg/250px-Mirror.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A mirror reflecting the image of a vase","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMirror.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Enhanced_aluminum_coated_first_surface_mirror_on_an_optical_flat.JPG/500px-Enhanced_aluminum_coated_first_surface_mirror_on_an_optical_flat.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Enhanced_aluminum_coated_first_surface_mirror_on_an_optical_flat.JPG/1280px-Enhanced_aluminum_coated_first_surface_mirror_on_an_optical_flat.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Enhanced_aluminum_coated_first_surface_mirror_on_an_optical_flat.JPG/500px-Enhanced_aluminum_coated_first_surface_mirror_on_an_optical_flat.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A first-surface mirror coated with aluminium and enhanced with dielectric coatings. The angle of the incident light (represented by both the light in the mirror and the shadow behind it) exactly matches the angle of reflection (the reflected light shining on the table).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEnhanced_aluminum_coated_first_surface_mirror_on_an_optical_flat.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/WW1AcousticMirrorKilnsea%28PaulGlazzard%29Jan2007.jpg/500px-WW1AcousticMirrorKilnsea%28PaulGlazzard%29Jan2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/WW1AcousticMirrorKilnsea%28PaulGlazzard%29Jan2007.jpg/500px-WW1AcousticMirrorKilnsea%28PaulGlazzard%29Jan2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"4.5-metre (15 ft)-tall acoustic mirror near Kilnsea Grange, East Yorkshire, UK, from World War I. The mirror magnified the sound of approaching enemy Zeppelins for a microphone placed at the focal point. Sound waves are much longer than light waves, thus the object produces diffuse reflections in the visual spectrum.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWW1AcousticMirrorKilnsea(PaulGlazzard)Jan2007.jpg"}],"sounds":{}}}